impression_en:
Peripheral lung cancer in the left lower lobe with slight reduction in solid component.
Multiple lymph nodes in the mediastinum, bilateral hila, and supraclavicular regions remain stable;
Left pleural irregular thickening remains stable.

Status post left upper lobectomy, with extensive pneumomediastinum and subcutaneous emphysema;
 Small left pneumothorax and bilateral pleural effusions.
 Multiple micronodules in both lungs remain unchanged.

Status post right lower lobectomy; small right pleural effusion is stable;
 Tiny pulmonary nodules and small mediastinal/hilar lymph nodes are stable, likely representing old changes;
 Bilateral adrenal mass is noted; please refer to abdominal imaging findings.

Postoperative status of the right upper lobe;
 The nodule in the right middle lobe has decreased in size.
 Lymph nodes in the mediastinum and right supraclavicular region are unchanged.

Cavitary mass in the right lower lobe, likely malignant;
 Multiple nodules in the right lower lobe, likely metastatic;
 Multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the hila and mediastinum, likely metastatic;
 Metastasis to the T12 vertebra.

Lesion in the lingular segment of the left upper lobe, consistent with lung cancer, with obstructive atelectasis;
 Multiple mediastinal lymph nodes, some enlarged, likely metastatic;
 Left pleural effusion and small pericardial effusion.
 Bilateral thyroid nodules; correlation with ultrasound is recommended.

Mass in the apicoposterior segment of the left upper lobe, lung cancer is highly likely;
Multiple lymph nodes in the hila and mediastinum, more likely benign; clinical correlation recommended.
Right lower lobe old lesion; small GGO in the right upper lobe, follow-up; irregular micro-nodule in the left lower lobe, nature undetermined.

Postoperative status after left upper lobectomy;
 Stable nodule along the right horizontal fissure and stable small ground-glass nodules in the right lower lobe.
 The low-density subcarinal nodule has slightly decreased in size.

Status post partial resection of the right upper lobe;
 Enlargement of some small nodules in the right lung, with a high possibility of metastasis;
 Enlarged left axillary lymph node, consistent with metastasis;
 Partial compression fracture of the T12 vertebral body.

Postoperative status after left upper lobectomy;
 Stable nodule along the right horizontal fissure and stable small ground-glass nodules in the right lower lobe.
 The low-density subcarinal nodule has slightly increased in size.

Irregular mass in the left upper lobe, suspicious for malignancy;
 Multiple nodules and masses in both lungs, suspicious for metastasis;
 Small amount of left-sided pleural effusion.

Soft-tissue mass in the left lower lobe—peripheral lung cancer is highly likely;
 Enlarged lymph nodes in the left hilum and mediastinal station 4L—metastasis is possible.
 Bulla in the right middle lobe.

The extent of the surrounding ground-glass component of the irregular nodule in the right upper lobe appears slightly increased;
 the ground-glass opacity nodule in the left upper lobe appears slightly increased;
 the remainder is stable.

Mass in the right lower lobe, suggestive of peripheral lung cancer;
 Multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the right hilum, likely metastatic;
 Tiny nodule in the right lower lobe, possibly chronic? Recommend follow-up.

Nodule in the right upper lobe, likely malignant, with high possibility of peripheral lung cancer;
 Multiple nodules in both lungs and subpleurally, likely metastatic;
 Enlarged right hilar lymph node, likely metastatic.

1. Right middle lobe mass highly suspicious for malignancy with invasion of the horizontal fissure pleura;
2. Lesion in the dorsal segment of the right lower lobe likely chronic inflammation, though malignancy cannot be excluded;
3. Multiple small nodules in the right interlobar pleura and small right pleural effusion, suggestive of pleural seeding;
4. Right hilar region likely contains enlarged lymph nodes.

1. Small faint ground-glass opacity in the right lower lobe remains unchanged;
2. Near resolution of consolidation in the anterior basal segment of the right lower lobe; increased extent of lesion in the lateral basal segment and increased number of peribronchial small punctate opacities, suggesting progression of inflammatory disease; correlate with clinical status and short-term follow-up after treatment;
3. Chronic changes in the right middle and upper lobes remain unchanged.

Status post left mastectomy;
 Multiple small nodules in the left lung, unchanged, for follow-up;
 Possible inflammatory lesion in the left lower lobe, for follow-up.

Status post right lower lobectomy;
Stable pulmonary micronodules;
Left adrenal mass noted; correlate with abdominal imaging.

Rounded mass in the left lower lobe, suggestive of peripheral lung cancer on the left;
 Enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes, likely metastatic;
 Multiple small nodules in the right lung, indeterminate in nature; suggest follow-up.

Irregular mass in the right upper lobe, likely peripheral lung cancer;
 Multiple small nodules in both lungs, likely metastatic;
 Multiple enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes, likely metastatic.

Right hilar mass consistent with right central lung cancer;
 Multiple small nodules in the right lung, likely metastatic;
 Enlarged mediastinal lymph node, likely metastatic;
 Small right pleural effusion.

Status post esophagogastric anastomosis;
 Small pulmonary nodules are largely stable, multiple old changes in both lungs.
 Small nodule in the left back is stable, likely benign; recommend follow-up.

Status post surgery at the right apical pleura, with small amount of local gas and fluid;
 Small nodule in the right upper lobe unchanged;
 Right supraclavicular lymph node, recommend follow-up.

Mass in the left lower lobe, suggestive of peripheral lung cancer;
 Type 3 pulmonary tuberculosis in the right upper lobe; multiple clustered tree-in-bud nodules in both lungs, possibly representing disseminated tuberculosis?
 Multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the mediastinum and left hilum; tuberculosis-related lymphadenopathy versus metastasis—differential diagnosis advised; clinical correlation recommended.

Peripheral lung cancer in the left lower lobe has decreased in size compared to prior study;
Multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the mediastinum, bilateral hila, and supraclavicular regions have decreased in size;
Left pleural irregular thickening has improved compared to prior study.

Postoperative changes in the right lung.
Multiple tiny nodules in both lungs, suggestive of metastases;
Multiple lymph nodes in the mediastinum, bilateral hila, and right supraclavicular region, metastasis? Further investigation recommended.

Status post left lower lobectomy;
Stable solitary small nodule in the right lung;
Lesion in the right lobe of the liver noted; refer to abdominal imaging findings.

1. Small faint ground-glass opacity in the right lower lobe remains unchanged;
2. Improvement of inflammation in the lateral basal segment of the right lower lobe; follow-up recommended;
3. Chronic changes in the right middle and upper lobes remain unchanged.

Mass in the left upper lobe, consistent with peripheral lung cancer;
 Small nodules in the left lung and interlobar pleura, likely metastatic;
 Multiple enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes, likely metastatic.

Status post left mastectomy;
 Multiple small nodules in the left lung, likely metastatic;
 Recommend abdominal evaluation to exclude lesion in the left kidney.

Irregular nodule in the right upper lobe, likely peripheral lung cancer; 
multiple ground-glass nodules in both lungs, nature undetermined; 
enlargement of the right hilar lymph node, possible metastasis? Follow-up recommended.

Multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the subcarinal region and bilateral hila; metastasis? Recommend follow-up.
 Atelectasis of the right middle lobe;
 Multifocal lung lesions, likely chronic in nature.

Status post right upper lobectomy;
new nodule in the right lower lobe, metastasis should be considered;
stable nodules in the remaining lung and pleura, likely old lesions;
low-density hepatic nodule, gallstones, and bilateral adrenal nodules—correlate with abdominal imaging.

Status post right upper lobectomy; 
 Soft tissue thickening in the right hilar region and inhomogeneous hydropneumothorax in the right upper pleural cavity, consistent with infectious changes; 
 Multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the mediastinum and hila, likely inflammatory/reactive; metastatic disease cannot be excluded and should be differentiated with preoperative imaging; 
 Nodules in both lower lobes, likely chronic, recommend follow-up observation.

Postoperative changes in the left lung.
 Ground-glass opacity in the right upper lobe is essentially unchanged;
 Fatty liver.

Mass in the right lower hilum, likely representing right central lung cancer; bronchoscopy is recommended;
 Scattered small patchy hazy opacities in the right lower lobe, likely inflammatory; correlation with clinical findings is recommended;
 Nature of the tiny nodule in the right upper lobe is undetermined; follow-up is recommended.

Status post right lower lobectomy; small right pleural effusion is stable;
 Tiny pulmonary nodules and small mediastinal/hilar lymph nodes are stable, likely representing old changes;
 Bilateral adrenal mass is noted; please refer to abdominal imaging findings.

Circumferential thickening of the gastric wall in the thoracic stomach—recurrence? Gastroscopy is recommended;
New micro-nodules in both lungs, nature uncertain—metastasis? Follow-up is recommended;
New small patchy hazy opacities in both lower lobes, likely inflammatory—correlation with clinical findings is advised;
New small pericardial effusion.

Cavitary mass in the right lower lobe, likely malignant;
 Multiple nodules in the right lower lobe, likely metastatic;
 Multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the hila and mediastinum, likely metastatic;
 Metastasis to the T12 vertebra.

Upper thoracic esophageal wall mass consistent with esophageal carcinoma; the small anterior lymph node likely represents metastasis; 
 Old tuberculosis in the left lung; right lower lobe atelectasis likely due to chronic changes with bronchial mucus plug. 
 Ascites and marked splenomegaly; abdominal evaluation is recommended.

Follow-up of esophageal carcinoma, with reduced wall thickness;
 Mediastinal group 4 lymph node has decreased in size, suggestive of metastasis;
 Most of the multiple small nodules in both lungs have decreased in size, consistent with metastasis.

Irregular mass adjacent to the mediastinum in the basal segment of the right lower lobe, lung cancer cannot be excluded; recommend bronchoscopy;
Multiple small nodules in the right lung, nature undetermined.
Multiple bronchiectasis in the right lung with inflammatory changes.

Irregular mediastinal-adjacent mass in the left upper lobe, considered for mediastinal-type lung cancer;
 Scattered tiny nodules in both lungs, old lesions? Recommend follow-up;
 Slightly enlarged lymph node in the right lower hilum, recommend follow-up.

Status post right upper lobectomy;
 Enlarging nodule in the right middle lobe, consistent with metastasis; mediastinal lymph nodes stable;
 Benign nodule in the left upper lobe stable.

1. Post-esophagogastric anastomosis with thickening of the anastomosis; recommend follow-up;
2. Stable small right hilar and mediastinal lymph nodes.
3. Increased soft tissue density around the surgical suture in the right lower lobe compared to prior; further evaluation recommended.

Post-left lower lobectomy;
 Right lower lobe nodule unchanged; no new pulmonary mass;
 Increased bone density in L1 vertebral body, recommend further evaluation to exclude metastasis.

Nature of the irregular nodule in the right upper lobe is undetermined; peripheral lung cancer is highly likely; 
multiple enlarged lymph nodes at mediastinal stations 4R and 11R, possible metastasis? 
Scattered linear opacities and indistinct micronodules in the right upper lobe and left lower lobe, likely old foci.

Cavitary mass in the right lower lobe, consistent with central lung cancer on the right;
 Enlarged right hilar lymph node, likely metastatic; recommend follow-up;
 Focal pleural thickening with small right pleural effusion.

Postoperative status of right lower lobectomy; soft tissue density at the bronchial stump is likely postoperative change;
 Multiple pulmonary nodules, possible metastases; comparison with prior films is recommended;
 Slight enlargement of the subcarinal lymph node; follow-up is recommended.

Postoperative changes in the left back.
Postoperative changes after right upper lobectomy; all previously seen multiple nodules in both lungs have disappeared; further evaluation recommended;
Right pleural thickening has significantly improved;
Mediastinal lymph nodes have decreased in size.

Consolidation in the right lower lobe, possibly inflammatory; consider clinical correlation;
Indeterminate nature of the ground-glass nodule in the right upper lobe;
Indeterminate nature of scattered tiny nodules in both lungs; recommend follow-up.

Large irregular mass in the right hilum, suggestive of central lung cancer on the right; 
 Multiple enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes, likely metastatic; 
 Right pleural effusion.

Irregular mass in the right hilum, consistent with right central lung cancer;
 Multiple nodules in both lungs, likely metastatic;
 Multiple enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes, likely metastatic.

Irregular wall thickening and enhancement of the mid-thoracic esophagus, consistent with esophageal carcinoma;
Enlarged mediastinal lymph node, likely metastatic;
Small indistinct nodule in the left upper lobe, likely old lesion.

Wall thickening and enhancement in the upper thoracic esophagus, lower esophagus, and cardia, consistent with carcinoma; recommend upper gastrointestinal contrast study;
 Enlarged right supraclavicular lymph node, likely metastatic;
 Scattered tiny pulmonary nodules, indeterminate nature, recommend follow-up.

Right middle lobe carcinoma and multiple small lung nodules are stable;
 Subcarinal metastatic lymph node is markedly enlarged; other lymph nodes are stable or decreased in size;
 Left thyroid lobe nodule has increased in size.

Irregular wall thickening and enhancement of the upper and middle thoracic esophagus, consistent with esophageal carcinoma;
 Multiple pulmonary nodules, likely metastatic;
 Multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the right supraclavicular and mediastinal regions, likely metastatic.

Subpleural ground-glass opacity nodule in the apical segment of the right upper lobe, suspicious for malignancy;
 Slightly hyperdense nodule in the right upper lobe, likely representing a chronic lesion;
 Enlargement of some mediastinal lymph nodes, requires follow-up;
 Hyperdense nodule in the right 8th posterior rib, indeterminate in nature; clinical correlation and bone scan are recommended;
 Subcutaneous nodule in the left chest wall, likely benign; clinical correlation advised.

Right middle lobe nodule, with high suspicion for malignancy; 
Multiple enlarged lymph nodes at the right hilum and mediastinum, warranting further evaluation; 
Left upper lobe GGO and small nodule in the right lower lobe, warranting follow-up; 
Fibrous bands in the right lung and multiple bullae in both lungs.

Postoperative status of the left upper lobe mass;
 Ground-glass opacity in the right upper lobe has disappeared, new small patchy hazy shadow in the right upper lobe, possibly inflammatory, suggest correlation with clinical findings.
 Soft tissue shadow in the right hilum slightly thicker than before, suggest follow-up.

Post esophago-gastric anastomosis; left tracheoesophageal groove mass, likely lymph node metastasis, correlate clinically;
 Lung nodules unchanged.
 Stable pleural thickening, reduced pleural effusion, improved lung expansion, and small pneumothorax on the right.

Postoperative changes after esophagectomy; no evidence of recurrence or metastasis in the chest;
 Small nodule in the left upper lobe is stable, likely benign. Follow up.
 Small pericardial effusion is unchanged.

Slight improvement in wall thickening of the distal thoracic esophagus compared to prior, consistent with post-treatment changes of esophageal carcinoma;
 Multiple mediastinal and upper abdominal lymph nodes are slightly smaller than before;
 Tiny nodule in the right interlobar pleura is stable; continue observation.

Postoperative status in the right upper lobe;
 Right hilar mass and multiple mediastinal lymph nodes, both increased in size, likely metastatic;
 Decreased density in the right 4th rib lesion and multiple fracture lines in the right 5th rib.

Postoperative changes following right upper lobectomy;
 Multiple tiny nodules in the left lung have increased in size—metastatic disease is considered.
 Hepatic and right renal cysts—please correlate with abdominal imaging.

Focal eccentric wall thickening in the distal esophagus; further evaluation is recommended to differentiate between a mass and a hiatal hernia;
 Ground-glass opacity in the left lower lobe, indeterminate in nature;
 Scattered small nodules in both lungs, likely represent old foci.

Round nodule in the right lower lobe, consistent with peripheral lung cancer;
 Multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the left axilla, mediastinum, and right hilum, likely metastatic;
 Multiple tiny subpleural nodules in the right lower lobe, follow-up recommended.

1. Small faint ground-glass opacity in the right lower lobe remains unchanged;
2. Reduction of consolidation in the lateral basal segment of the right lower lobe; small nodular opacities are largely unchanged;
3. Chronic changes in the right middle and upper lobes remain unchanged.

Cavitary mass in the right upper lobe, likely lung cancer; 
Multiple lung lesions likely metastatic, though some may represent exudative inflammation; 
Bilateral moderate pleural effusion; 
Extensive lymph node metastases in the right supraclavicular, mediastinal, and bilateral hilar regions, with invasion into the superior vena cava forming a tumor thrombus; 
Lytic lesion in the second lumbar vertebra likely metastatic.

Mass in the medial basal segment of the left lower lobe, likely malignant;
Mediastinal lymph nodes, possible metastasis.
Irregular small nodule in the right lung, indeterminate nature; recommend follow-up.

Status post cervical esophagogastric anastomosis; anastomotic leak with skin sinus tract formation unchanged;
 anastomosis-bronchus fistula; large area of aspiration pneumonia in the left lower lobe; right upper thoracic anastomotic leak unchanged.
 Multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the mediastinum and hilum, some unchanged, some increased in size.

Status post esophagogastric anastomosis;
 Reduction in bilateral pleural effusions and improvement in lung atelectasis.
 Multiple liver and bilateral adrenal masses unchanged, likely benign; correlate with abdominal imaging.

Postoperative changes following esophagectomy. 
Small bilateral pleural effusions; 
postoperative changes in the right ribs.

Left upper lobe mass, considered malignant;
Right upper lobe nodule, indeterminate; chronic inflammation cannot be excluded;
Suspected multiple lymph node metastases in the bilateral supraclavicular regions, left axilla, and mediastinum.

1. Mid-thoracic esophageal mass, consistent with esophageal carcinoma;
2. Enlarged lymph nodes in the right hilum, mediastinum, and along the gastric fundus, metastasis cannot be excluded;
3. Old tuberculosis in the right upper lobe, minimal old changes elsewhere;
4. Subcutaneous nodule in the left anterior chest wall; correlate clinically with physical examination;
5. Dense focus in T10 vertebral body, likely benign.

Round-like mass in the left lingular lobe, suggestive of peripheral lung cancer; 
Scattered small nodules in the right lung, likely metastatic; 
Enlarged lymph node at the left hilum, likely metastatic.

Postoperative status after left upper lobectomy;
 Stable nodule along the right horizontal fissure and stable small ground-glass nodules in the right lower lobe.
 Stable low-density subcarinal nodule.

Status post esophagogastric anastomosis;
 Multiple linear and reticular opacities in the left lung likely due to radiation fibrosis;
 Increased soft tissue beneath the carina and new small nodule in the left lung are suspicious for metastasis, but reactive post-radiation changes cannot be excluded; recommend correlation with clinical history and interval follow-up.
 Multiple hepatic nodules have increased in size; recommend abdominal imaging.
